# Architecture-Tracked Project: {{projectName}}

This project uses a living architecture document, `{{architectureFile}}`. You — the AI coding agent — are responsible for keeping it in sync with the code.

> **arkive is installed.** You have access to an MCP server (`arkive serve --stdio`) and web viewer. Use MCP tools when available; fall back to direct file editing when not.

## The Three Rules

1. **On every session start**: Read `{{architectureFile}}` (or `architecture://session-context`). It is your memory across sessions, compaction, and model switches.
2. **Before any major change**: Re-read `{{architectureFile}}` to learn what exists and why, and read the decisions attached to the part you are about to touch.
3. **After any major change**: Run `npx @ericjdz/arkive reconcile --apply`, append a `history` entry, and record the decision if one was made.

**Before claiming any task is complete**: re-run `reconcile --apply`. Confirm the derived half matches the code and the authored half carries every choice you made. If either does not, you are not done.

## What Counts as a Major Change

- Adding, removing, or replacing a service, datastore, or external API
- Changing how components communicate (new protocols, new endpoints)
- Adding or modifying infrastructure (hosting, CI/CD, caching, CDN)
- Adopting a new framework or major library for a significant function
- Refactoring that changes module boundaries or data flow

## What you write, and what you derive

**Structure is derived. You author intent.** A transcribed edge is a claim nothing checks, and it drifts from the first commit.

| Derive — never hand-write | Author — nothing else can observe it |
|---|---|
| `nodes[]` for modules and external packages | `summary` — what a node is *for* |
| `edges[]` of kind `imports` / `calls` | `owner` — a person decided this |
| Anything the scan can cite a `file:line` for | `stack`, `dataFlows` |
| | `service` / `datastore` / `endpoint` nodes — boundaries the code does not declare |
| | `derives_from` edges, and `note` on any element |
| | `decisions[]` — the artifact this product exists to preserve |

## How to Update

### 1. Derive the structure
```bash
npx @ericjdz/arkive reconcile --apply
```

### 2. Record the intent (MCP preferred)
- `update_architecture` — add or remove `nodes`, `edges`, `decisions`, `dataFlows`, `stack`. A decision sent in the same call attaches to everything that call created, so you never maintain the link by hand.
- `record_decision` — a decision with rationale, a rejected alternative, and the nodes/edges it explains via `subjects`.
- `why_does_this_exist` — resolve a node id to the decisions that explain it, with each claim's provenance.
- `check_drift` — static analysis comparing the document against the code.

### 3. Record the intent (direct file editing, if MCP is unavailable)
1. Read `{{architectureFile}}`
2. Add/update `nodes[]` / `edges[]` **only** where derivation cannot reach, each with `"provenance": { "derived": false, "authoredBy": "agent" }`
3. Add entries to `decisions[]` — MUST include at least one rejected alternative with a non-empty `whyNot`
4. Append to `history` with `timestamp`, `action`, `summary`
5. Write the complete valid JSON back

## Schema Reference

### Node
```json
{
  "id": "kebab-case-id",
  "kind": "module|service|datastore|external|datastructure|endpoint",
  "name": "Human Readable Name",
  "summary": "What this node is for. \"\" is honest when nothing observed one.",
  "tech": "optional",
  "path": "src/schema (meaningful for module, optional)",
  "owner": "optional",
  "provenance": { "derived": true, "rule": "ts-js/module-node", "evidence": ["src/schema/graph.ts:1"] }
}
```

### Edge
```json
{
  "id": "kebab-case-id",
  "from": "<node-id>",
  "to": "<node-id>",
  "kind": "imports|calls|reads|writes|derives_from",
  "label": "HTTP/REST",
  "note": "optional human note",
  "provenance": { "derived": true, "rule": "ts-js/import-edge", "evidence": ["src/cli/index.ts:5"] }
}
```

`label` is transport naming only and is never semantic — `kind` is. `decided_by` is computed from `decision.subjects` and is never stored.

### Provenance — exactly one shape, required on every node and edge
```json
{ "derived": true,  "rule": "<rule-id>", "evidence": ["src/x.ts:42"] }
{ "derived": false, "authoredBy": "agent|human", "reviewedAt": "<ISO 8601>" }
```

**No `confidence` field exists and you must not add one.** It was declared on every v1 service and connection, read by two call sites, and written by no code path — a number an LLM assigns to itself is not evidence. `evidence: ["file:line"]` is, because anyone can open it.

### Decision (the most important — never skip)
```json
{
  "id": "dec-NNN",
  "topic": "What the decision is about",
  "decision": "What you chose",
  "rationale": "WHY you chose it — specific to this project's needs",
  "alternatives": [
    { "name": "Alternative A", "whyNot": "Why this was rejected" }
  ],
  "timestamp": "2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
  "subjects": [
    { "kind": "node", "ref": "kebab-case-id" }
  ]
}
```

At least one alternative must carry a non-empty `whyNot` — a record with no rejected alternative is an observation, not a decision. `subjects` names **nodes and edges**; the ids are the same strings the old `relatedServices` field used. A subject that no longer resolves is reported, never deleted.

### History entry (append on every change)
```json
{
  "timestamp": "<ISO 8601>",
  "action": "reconciliation|architecture_decision|refactor|...",
  "summary": "One-line summary",
  "agent": "optional"
}
```

Capped at 50 entries; a truncated document carries `"historyTruncated": true` and keeps saying so until a human removes it.

The document's discriminator is `"schemaVersion": 2`. There is no v1 `version` string, and a document carrying one is a v1 file this build refuses to write over.

## The fields you must not write

`services[]` and `connections[]` may appear in `{{architectureFile}}`. They are a **derived view**, computed from `nodes[]` / `edges[]` on every read and rewritten from the graph on every write. `decisions[].relatedServices` is likewise computed from the `kind: "node"` subjects.

Anything you put in them is folded back into the graph and then replaced by the recomputed projection on the next write — silently. Send `nodes` and `edges` instead.

## Validation Rules

1. Node, edge, and decision ids are unique.
2. Every `edge.from` and `edge.to` must reference an existing node. An endpoint that resolves to nothing is preserved as `dangling: true` and reported, not dropped.
3. Every derived element cites `evidence` with a `file:line` someone can open.
4. All timestamps must be ISO 8601.

## CLI Commands

| Command | Action |
|---|---|
| `npx @ericjdz/arkive reconcile --apply` | Derive structure from the code and write it |
| `npx @ericjdz/arkive reconcile` | Report drift without writing anything |
| `npx @ericjdz/arkive watch` | Re-derive on every change |
| `npx @ericjdz/arkive serve` | Start MCP server + web viewer |
| `npx @ericjdz/arkive serve --stdio` | MCP server only (for agent integration) |
| `npx @ericjdz/arkive status` | Counts, schema version, drift verdict |
| `npx @ericjdz/arkive export <markdown\|mermaid>` | Export as markdown or Mermaid |
| `npx @ericjdz/arkive learn` | Web viewer in Learning Mode |
| `npx @ericjdz/arkive install <platform>` | Register MCP server in your AI platform config |

## MCP Auto-Install

To register the arkive MCP server in your platform, ask the user which platform they use, then run:

```bash
npx @ericjdz/arkive install <platform>
```

Supported platforms: `claude-desktop`, `claude-code`, `cursor`, `opencode`, `vscode`, `windsurf`, `cline`

The command writes the correct config entry for the chosen platform. The user must restart their agent session afterward.